WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.403
MuSc is a zero-shot method for industrial anomaly
detection and segmentation,

00:00:06.483 --> 00:00:11.143
with no training, no prompts, and no normal
reference images.

00:00:11.223 --> 00:00:16.815
Its insight: unlabeled test images hold enough cues
to score one another.

00:00:16.895 --> 00:00:20.700
Published at ICLR 2024, it tops MVTec AD and VisA.

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:26.421
Detecting defects is a core vision task, but
existing methods are demanding.

00:00:26.501 --> 00:00:30.844
One-class approaches need a bank of normal images
per product;

00:00:30.924 --> 00:00:34.981
CLIP-based zero-shot methods rely on hand-written prompts.

00:00:35.061 --> 00:00:36.978
Many factories have neither.

00:00:37.058 --> 00:00:41.900
The goal: segment anomalies with no training,
prompts, or references.

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:44.918
The authors make a simple observation.

00:00:44.998 --> 00:00:48.565
In a batch of unlabeled test images of one product,

00:00:48.645 --> 00:00:52.856
a normal patch finds many similar patches across
the others,

00:00:52.936 --> 00:00:55.287
because normal appearance repeats.

00:00:55.367 --> 00:00:59.507
An abnormal patch finds only a few, since defects
are rare.

00:00:59.587 --> 00:01:03.440
That asymmetry is a signal already inside the test set.

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:07.772
MuSc is a training-free, prompt-free pipeline with
three pieces.

00:01:07.852 --> 00:01:11.803
First, local neighborhood aggregation at multiple degrees,

00:01:11.883 --> 00:01:15.835
representing each patch at several scales to
capture tiny and large defects.

00:01:15.915 --> 00:01:19.867
Second, mutual scoring, where every test image
scores every other.

00:01:19.947 --> 00:01:23.898
Third,

00:01:23.978 --> 00:01:27.930
re-scoring with a constrained image-level
neighborhood that cleans the final decision.

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:31.174
The backbone is a frozen vision transformer.

00:01:31.254 --> 00:01:38.335
Patch tokens from several stages are aggregated at
multiple neighborhood degrees to represent defect sizes.

00:01:38.415 --> 00:01:43.822
In mutual scoring, each patch is scored by its
nearest match in every other image,

00:01:43.902 --> 00:01:48.440
and only the smallest score interval is averaged,
sharpening the gap.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:53.860
The image score is refined with a constrained
neighborhood graph on class tokens.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:59.162
MuSc is evaluated on the two most-used industrial
benchmarks.

00:01:59.242 --> 00:02:04.493
MVTec AD spans fifteen categories, ten objects and
five textures.

00:02:04.573 --> 00:02:09.249
VisA covers twelve object categories across three domains.

00:02:09.329 --> 00:02:12.283
Both mix normal and defective images.

00:02:12.363 --> 00:02:15.318
Classification uses AUROC and F1-max;

00:02:15.398 --> 00:02:20.320
segmentation adds pixel-level metrics and
per-region overlap.

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:24.457
Results are striking for a label-free method.

00:02:24.537 --> 00:02:28.293
On MVTec AD,

00:02:28.373 --> 00:02:32.130
MuSc reaches ninety-seven point eight percent image
AUROC and lifts per-region overlap

00:02:32.210 --> 00:02:35.967
segmentation by over twenty-one points versus the
best prior zero-shot method.

00:02:36.047 --> 00:02:39.803
On VisA it gains over ten points.

00:02:39.883 --> 00:02:43.640
It even beats most four-shot methods and rivals
full-shot approaches.

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:46.079
Ablations confirm each choice.

00:02:46.159 --> 00:02:52.640
Combining three aggregation degrees works best:
small neighborhoods catch tiny VisA defects,

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:55.136
large ones catch big MVTec defects.

00:02:55.216 --> 00:03:00.128
Averaging the smallest thirty percent interval
beats the max or range,

00:03:00.208 --> 00:03:03.694
lifting AUROC to ninety-seven point eight percent.

00:03:03.774 --> 00:03:08.900
Re-scoring raises VisA image AUROC from ninety to
ninety-two point eight.

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:13.198
In headline terms,

00:03:13.278 --> 00:03:17.196
MuSc reaches ninety-seven point eight percent image
AUROC on MVTec AD and ninety-two point eight on VisA,

00:03:17.276 --> 00:03:21.194
with pixel AUROC of ninety-seven point three and
ninety-eight point eight.

00:03:21.274 --> 00:03:25.192
It adds over twenty-one points on MVTec
segmentation, all with no training, prompts,

00:03:25.272 --> 00:03:29.190
or references.

00:03:29.570 --> 00:03:34.114
The lasting idea: a set of unlabeled test images
can supervise itself.

00:03:34.194 --> 00:03:36.492
By letting images score one another,

00:03:36.572 --> 00:03:40.323
repeating normal structure is separated from rare defects,

00:03:40.403 --> 00:03:42.172
with no training or prompts,

00:03:42.252 --> 00:03:47.060
giving state-of-the-art zero-shot detection that
rivals full-shot methods.
